Auto-Start Tomcat on Server Reboot
To ensure Tomcat starts automatically when your server restarts, you need to register it as a system service. The exact steps depend on your OS. Here's how to do it for the most common setups.

🐧 Linux (systemd — RHEL, CentOS, Ubuntu, Debian)
This is the modern, recommended approach.

Step 1: Create a systemd service file

sudo vi /etc/systemd/system/tomcat.service
Paste the following (adjust JAVA_HOME, CATALINA_HOME, and User to match your environment):

[Unit]
Description=Apache Tomcat Web Application Container
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=forking

Environment=JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk
Environment=CATALINA_PID=/opt/tomcat/temp/tomcat.pid
Environment=CATALINA_HOME=/opt/tomcat
Environment=CATALINA_BASE=/opt/tomcat
Environment='CATALINA_OPTS=-Xms512M -Xmx1024M -server -XX:+UseParallelGC'

ExecStart=/opt/tomcat/bin/startup.sh
ExecStop=/opt/tomcat/bin/shutdown.sh

User=tomcat
Group=tomcat
UMask=0007
RestartSec=10
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Step 2: Reload systemd, enable & start
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable tomcat      # ← enables auto-start on boot
sudo systemctl start tomcat
sudo systemctl status tomcat      # verify it's running
Step 3: Test it
sudo reboot
# After server comes back:
sudo systemctl status tomcat
✅ Key flag: Restart=always also relaunches Tomcat if it crashes, not just on reboot.
